    1. The Ofgem January 2027 Price Cap Hike: Why Electricity Is Rising to 32.5p/kWh
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    Every quarter, Great Britain's energy regulator, <a href="https://www.ofgem.gov.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-emerald-700 font-bold hover:underline">Ofgem</a>, recalibrates the default tariff price cap that protects approximately 29 million households on standard variable tariffs (SVTs). Following a relatively stable autumn 2026 where electricity hovered at 24.5p–26.32p/kWh, leading energy market consultancies (including Cornwall Insight) have forecast a substantial 26% escalation beginning 1 January 2027.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    The primary driver behind the 32.54p/kWh surge is a severe escalation in wholesale European natural gas prices heading into mid-winter. Because natural gas-fired combined-cycle gas turbine (CCGT) power plants frequently set the marginal clearing price on Britain's National Grid ESO electricity market, elevated gas prices translate directly into higher electricity generation costs.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed">
    In addition, network operators are absorbing higher transmission and distribution charges, capital upgrades to accommodate renewable generation, and elevated grid balancing costs. While the UK Government has maintained 0% VAT on domestic electricity through 31 March 2027 to soften winter hardship, the sheer scale of the wholesale spike means unit rates will surge by over 6.2p per unit of power.
  </p>

