  <!-- Section 4: Public Rapid Charging Economics -->
  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight">
    4. Why UK Public Rapid Chargers (79p/kWh) Cost More Than Petrol in 2027
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    Notice the glaring red row in Table 2: drivers who rely exclusively on commercial public DC rapid chargers (50 kW to 350 kW, operated by networks such as InstaVolt, bp pulse, Gridserve, and IONITY) spend <strong>£1,808 per year (22.6p/mile)</strong>—costing <strong>£336 more per year than fueling a petrol car</strong>.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    Why are UK rapid chargers so expensive compared to domestic electricity? Three commercial pressures drive public rates upward:
  </p>

  <ul class="space-y-3 mb-6 pl-5 list-disc text-gray-800 leading-relaxed font-medium">
    <li><strong>The 20% Commercial VAT Penalty:</strong> While domestic electricity enjoys 0% VAT relief until March 2027 (and 5% standard domestic VAT thereafter), public charging networks are legally classified as commercial electricity and taxed at the full <strong>20% standard VAT rate</strong>. Automotive bodies like FairCharge and the RAC continue to petition HM Treasury to equalize VAT across domestic and public charging.</li>
    <li><strong>High Standing Charges &amp; Capacity Fees:</strong> Connecting a high-power charging hub (often requiring 2 to 5 Megawatts of grid capacity) incurs massive recurring standing charges and capital upgrade fees paid to Distribution Network Operators (DNOs).</li>
    <li><strong>Peak Demand Energy Procurement:</strong> Public chargers experience peak utilization between 11:00 AM and 6:00 PM—precisely when wholesale grid electricity is at its most expensive.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="mb-8 leading-relaxed">
    For a detailed analysis of public network tariffs, read our complete investigation into <a href="/blog/uk-public-ev-charging-network-prices-compared-2026" class="text-emerald-700 font-bold hover:underline">UK Public EV Charging Network Prices Compared (2026): Cheapest &amp; Priciest Rapid Chargers</a>. The golden rule for UK EV drivers remains the <strong>80/20 rule</strong>: complete 80% to 90% of your charging at home overnight, and reserve 79p/kWh rapid chargers exclusively for mid-journey motorway top-ups.
  </p>

